        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1970s, there were no home video game consoles. Home entertainment was limited to television and board games. In arcades, the first simple electronic machines had already appeared, but access for the general public was limited. The idea that people could play interactive games on their own television was completely new and commercially unexplored.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe concept was born in August 1966, when Ralph H. Baer, an engineer at Sanders Associates, conceived the idea of an interactive device connected to a television. Together with colleagues Bill Harrison and Bill Rusch, Baer built seven successive prototypes between 1966 and 1969. The seventh, named „Brown Box,” included game mechanics and a light gun. After negotiations with several manufacturers who declined, Magnavox signed the production agreement in January 1971. George Kent and the Magnavox team refined the design, dropping color display to reduce costs and simplify FCC approvals. The console operated on analog principles, with diode-transistor logic, and was powered by batteries or an AC adapter.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eMagnavox Odyssey was launched in September 1972 at a price of $99.95, equivalent to approximately $750 in today\u0027s values. Distribution was handled exclusively through the Magnavox dealer network, which created confusion among consumers — many incorrectly believed it only worked with Magnavox televisions. The catalog included 28 titles distributed across 11 circuit cards. Table Tennis was included with the console, and Shooting Gallery came with a light gun manufactured by Nintendo. The console also came with physical accessories: dice, play money, and tokens, turning the experience into a hybrid between a video game and a board game.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the first calendar year, 69,000 units were sold. By the time production ceased in 1975, the total reached between 350,000 and 367,000 units. International expansion was significant: in 1973 the console reached the United Kingdom, and in 1974 ten other countries, including Venezuela and Mexico. Nolan Bushnell from Atari saw a demonstration of Table Tennis on the Odyssey in May 1972 and was inspired to create Pong, which subsequently triggered a major lawsuit. Magnavox sued Atari in 1974, obtaining $1.5 million. Judge John Grady declared in 1977 that Baer\u0027s patent was „the pioneer patent of the art of video games.”\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eMagnavox Odyssey remains the world\u0027s first home video game console. Its legal impact far exceeded its commercial one: Magnavox and Sanders Associates won over $100 million from lawsuits and settlements with Atari, Coleco, Mattel, Activision, and Nintendo. Ralph Baer, recognized as the „Father of Video Games,” received the National Medal of Technology in 2004. The original prototypes are preserved at the Smithsonian Institution, and the console is part of the permanent collection of the Museum of Modern Art in New York.",
