        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the late 1970s, the portable gaming device market was dominated by single-game devices — simple LEDs or LCD screens with fixed games. Nintendo\u0027s Game \u0026 Watch had not yet appeared. The idea of a portable device with interchangeable games, similar to what the Atari 2600 had achieved for home consoles, was completely new. Milton Bradley and Smith Engineering saw the opportunity to create the world\u0027s first portable console with swappable cartridges.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Microvision featured an atypical architecture: the base unit did not include a CPU — the processor was mounted directly on the cartridge, along with the game code. The console functioned as a hardware interface, with an LCD screen, battery power, controls, and a piezo-electric speaker. The first units used Intel 8021 processors, consuming approximately 1 W and requiring two 9V batteries. The later switch to the TI TMS1100 reduced consumption to 110 mW, allowing operation with a single 9V battery. The 16×16 pixel LCD screen and 12-button keypad, plus the paddle wheel, defined the gaming experience.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Microvision was launched in November 1979 at a price of 49.99 USD — an affordable price for the general public. The official library included 12 titles: Block Buster, Connect Four, Pinball, Sea Duel, and Cosmic Hunter, among others. The first year generated significant revenue for Smith Engineering and validated interest in the modular handheld format a decade before the Game Boy.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Microvision faced three critical reliability issues: LCD screen degradation (the screen rot phenomenon), cartridge vulnerability to electrostatic discharge, and rapid keypad wear. During the 1979 Christmas season, the high return rate confirmed the manufacturing limitations of the first generation. The limited library — only 12 titles compared to hundreds available on home consoles — also restricted long-term adoption. The system was discontinued in 1981, after a short but relevant commercial period.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Microvision remains the world\u0027s first portable console with interchangeable cartridges, anticipating by exactly a decade what the Game Boy would achieve on a global scale in 1989. Nintendo studied the lessons of the Microvision to avoid the same compromises in developing the Game Boy — battery life, hardware durability, and interface simplicity. Today, the console is regarded as a technical pioneer with imperfect execution, but with a clear influence on modern handheld design.",
