        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1980s, the Atari 2600 dominated the home console market, but it was increasingly evident that its hardware, launched in 1977, could not compete with the new arcade titles of the era. The ColecoVision had appeared in 1982 and demonstrated that the public wanted more faithful arcade conversions. Atari urgently needed a successor to answer competitors Intellivision and ColecoVision, without abandoning its user base.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Atari 5200 SuperSystem was designed based on the architecture of the Atari 8-bit computers (400/800), which accelerated development but also raised costs. The MOS 6502C processor, 16 KB of RAM, and the ANTIC/GTIA video chipsets offered resolutions up to 320×192 and support for palettes of 128 or 256 colors — a noticeable improvement over the 2600. The 360-degree analog joystick was conceptually innovative, but the faulty self-centering mechanism — a rubber bellows instead of metal springs — made the control imprecise and unreliable, turning the controller into the system\u0027s main weakness.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Atari 5200 was launched in October 1982 at a price of 269 USD — more expensive than the ColecoVision and significantly more expensive than the 2600. The chosen pack-in title, Super Breakout, failed to demonstrate the system\u0027s graphical power, unlike Donkey Kong included with the ColecoVision. The launch was slow and initially limited to major retailers only, expanding nationally only in January 1983. The console was initially incompatible with the vast 2600 game library — a questionable strategic decision.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eWhen the 1983 video game crash hit the industry, the Atari 5200 was already vulnerable. Many third-party manufacturers withdrew their support, the library remained limited compared to the 2600, and the Atari 400 computer — cheaper and with the same capabilities plus a keyboard — internally undermined the console\u0027s position. The flexible internal circuits of the controllers were susceptible to malfunctions, making finding a functional controller a challenge even for today\u0027s collectors. Atari officially announced the discontinuation of production on May 21, 1984, after just 19 months on the market.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Atari 5200 remains in industry history as a case study of powerful hardware undermined by poor ergonomics and a flawed launch strategy. A later adapter added compatibility with Atari 2600 games — proof that the initial decision of incompatibility had been an error. The homebrew community and modern collectors have found creative solutions through controller adapters and multi-cartridges like the Multicard Ultimate SD, keeping the system active for new generations of enthusiasts.",
