        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1980s, the Atari 2600 was visibly aging compared to the new consoles of the era. ColecoVision had demonstrated that the public demanded more faithful arcade conversions, and Nintendo\u0027s NES was preparing to enter the American market. Atari, after the devastating crash of 1983, urgently needed a powerful successor to the 2600 that could directly rival the new competitors.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Atari 7800 ProSystem was developed by General Computer Corporation under the coordination of Steve Golson, with the objective of rapidly surpassing the limitations of the 2600 and 5200 models. The platform was initially known internally as the Atari 3600. The MARIA chip at 7.16 MHz could handle up to 100 sprites and used a palette of 256 colors, with 25 displayable on a single scanline. A major point of differentiation: native backward compatibility with nearly all Atari 2600 cartridges. The audio remained based on the TIA for cost and compatibility reasons, with the option of adding the POKEY chip directly into cartridges for titles that required more advanced sound.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eAlthough the console had been announced in 1984, the change of ownership between Warner and Atari Corporation blocked the effective launch for nearly two years, keeping already-produced units in storage. When the system reached the market at large scale in 1986, the NES had already gained solid positions in retail. The launch price of 140 USD was competitive, and compatibility with 2600 games represented an important argument, but modest marketing limited the product\u0027s exposure.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Atari 7800 failed to recover the ground lost to the NES and Master System, even though it was a profitable product for the company due to low production costs. Third-party support was limited by Nintendo\u0027s exclusivity agreements that restricted studios from publishing simultaneously on competing platforms. The official withdrawal came at the beginning of 1992, after approximately 6 years of market presence.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe Atari 7800 validated the concept of backward compatibility as a real strategic argument — an important first for the industry. The console demonstrated that a platform can offer value through access to an older library, not just through new games. The digital signature mechanism for cartridges, which limited unauthorized software, anticipated the protection systems of later generations. In the current retro ecosystem, the persistent interest in the 7800 confirms that its technical design had value beyond its initial commercial performance.",
