        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the mid-1980s, the console market was split between the NES in Japan and North America and the Master System in Europe. Both were 8-bit architectures, and the arcade games of the era increasingly exceeded their graphical capabilities. Hudson Soft, a software developer experienced in arcade ports, and NEC, a giant in the electronics industry, saw the opportunity to launch a compact yet technically capable console that could rival systems marketed as ”16-bit”.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe PC Engine uses the HuC6280 processor, derived from the 6502/65C02 family, with two frequency modes: 1.79 MHz and 7.16 MHz. Although the CPU was 8-bit, the graphics subsystem consisting of the HuC6270 (VDC) and HuC6260 (VCE) configured a hybrid solution with 16-bit-level capabilities: 64 KB VRAM, resolutions between 256×224 and 512×240, 512 colors with 482 displayable simultaneously, and 64 sprites. The 6-channel PSG with stereo panning and PCM playback completed a flexible audio profile. The HuC6280 also integrated an MMU that extended addressing to 21 bits, allowing access to up to 2 MB of physical memory.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe PC Engine was launched in Japan in October 1987, quickly becoming a serious competitor to the Famicom. The case dimensions — remarkably compact for the era — were a visible differentiator. The console introduced the CD-ROM as an optional storage medium through the CD-ROM² expansion, opening possibilities for more elaborate games. In North America, the relaunch under the TurboGrafx-16 name arrived later and with less effective marketing, reducing its impact.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn Japan, the PC Engine managed to outpace the Famicom for a period and built an important library, especially in the shooter genre. The hardware ecosystem grew rapidly: CoreGrafx, SuperGrafx, TurboExpress, and Duo models offered flexibility but also introduced fragmentation that confused consumers. Regional locking of HuCards limited access to imports. In the West, the delayed market entry and unclear marketing messages undermined the console\u0027s chances.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe PC Engine pioneered the introduction of the CD-ROM as a storage medium for consoles — an innovation that would define the next generation of hardware. The console demonstrates that hybrid architecture can transcend the limits of standard ”8-bit” or ”16-bit” classifications. Its value remains relevant to collectors, supported by modern access solutions through emulation, FPGA replication, and flash carts on original hardware.",
