        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1990s, home consoles — including Super Nintendo and Sega Genesis — offered gaming experiences significantly inferior to the top arcade titles of the era. Arcade ports involved inevitable graphical and gameplay compromises. SNK, an arcade manufacturer with hit titles like Fatal Fury and Art of Fighting, decided to eliminate this gap by creating a unique system: identical hardware in both the arcade cabinet (MVS) and the home console (AES).\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo was designed in two complementary formats: MVS for commercial operators and AES for home users, both using exactly the same hardware. The technical configuration combined a Motorola 68000 at 12 MHz with a Zilog Z80 at 4 MHz for sound. The system operated at 320×224 with a palette of 65,536 colors, displaying up to 3,840 simultaneously, plus up to 380 sprites and real-time scaling. Cartridges reached 330 Mb initially and later over 700 Mb — unimaginable sizes for other consoles of the era. Neo Geo was marketed as \u002724-bit\u0027, reflecting the graphics data bus.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo AES was launched in Japan in 1990, initially conceived for rental due to high production costs. Market demand quickly transformed it into a premium console. The US launch at $649.99, alongside games at $200–300, clearly defined the platform\u0027s luxury positioning. MVS success in arcades sustained the ecosystem long-term and justified continued development investments.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo offered what no other home console could: authentic arcade-to-home parity. The King of Fighters, Samurai Shodown, Fatal Fury, and Metal Slug series defined the fighting genre and high-quality 2D action for a decade. MVS received official support until 2004. Neo Geo CD attempted to reduce software costs through CD-ROM, but the single-speed drive produced excessive loading times. Hyper Neo Geo 64 failed to achieve the planned transition to 3D.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo AES remains the symbol of the uncompromising premium console. It demonstrated that a specialized audience will pay significantly more for arcade authenticity. The collector market for Neo Geo has grown steadily, with rare cartridges reaching considerable sums. The brand continued through Neo Geo Mini and Arcade Stick Pro, and SNK series — Metal Slug, King of Fighters — continue to be released on modern platforms.",
