        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1990s, Atari Corporation was in decline, with Atari Lynx underperforming and no competitive home console. Nintendo and Sega dominated the market with SNES and Genesis. The market was preparing for the transition to a new generation, and Atari saw a window of opportunity to launch the first 64-bit console before Sony or Sega were ready.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eJaguar was developed by Flare Technology (Martin Brennan and John Mathieson) in the early 1990s. The project progressed so rapidly that Atari cancelled the Panther console to focus exclusively on Jaguar. The system used five processors distributed across three chips: Tom at 26.591 MHz contained a 32-bit RISC GPU, a 64-bit object processor, and a 64-bit Blitter; Jerry at the same frequency included a 32-bit DSP for 16-bit stereo sound; Motorola 68000 at 13.295 MHz acted as system manager. The 64-bit data bus justified the \u002764-bit\u0027 claim, although the main processors executed instructions at 32 or 16 bits — an immediate controversy.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eJaguar debuted on November 23, 1993, at $249.95, through market tests in New York and San Francisco, followed by the national launch in 1994. The marketing message \u0027Do the Math!\u0027 presented Jaguar as the first 64-bit console. IBM received a $500 million manufacturing contract. Tempest 2000 was almost universally considered the platform\u0027s best game, and Alien vs. Predator was the best-seller with 52,223 units.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe library was limited to 63 licensed titles — 50 on cartridge and 13 on CD. Many titles looked like 16-bit games, failing to demonstrate the hardware\u0027s power. The extremely complex multi-chip architecture, hardware bugs, and incomplete development tools discouraged major studios. The absence of developers like Capcom or Konami undermined the offering. In 1995, Jaguar held virtually 0% market share in the 32/64-bit segment. Production ceased in November 1995.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn 1999, Hasbro released Jaguar patents into the public domain, transforming it into an open platform for homebrew. Today, the active community and modern emulation (BigPEmu) keep the platform relevant for collectors. Jaguar remains in industry history as an example of the negative consequences of architecture too complex to program and a rushed launch without a solid library.",
