        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1990s, the console market was dominated by SNES and Genesis. Sony, a consumer electronics manufacturer with no video game experience, was working on a CD-ROM peripheral for Super Famicom alongside Nintendo — a partnership that spectacularly collapsed in 1991 when Nintendo chose Philips as an alternative. Sony president Norio Ohga decided that the public humiliation justified direct entry into the console market, not as a partner, but as a competitor.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eKen Kutaragi led PlayStation\u0027s development, establishing Sony Computer Entertainment Inc. in November 1993. The 32-bit R3000A RISC processor at 33.86 MHz, 2 MB RAM, and 1 MB VRAM formed the platform\u0027s core. The GPU processed 360,000 polygons per second and supported 16.7 million colors. Audio offered 16-bit across 24 channels. The controller design went through over 200 prototypes, arriving at the shape with handles inspired by airplane joysticks and geometric symbols — triangle, square, circle, X — instead of letters. The choice of CD-ROM as storage medium offered 650 MB versus 12 MB on cartridges and radically lower production costs.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePlayStation debuted in Japan on December 3, 1994. At E3 1995, Steve Race stepped on stage and spoke a single word: \u0027299\u0027 — the price in dollars, $100 less than Sega Saturn, triggering an instant competitive advantage. The North American launch followed in September 1995, and Europe in September 1995. Ridge Racer and Battle Arena Toshinden immediately demonstrated the platform\u0027s 3D capabilities.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePlayStation sold over 102 million units — the first console to surpass the 100 million milestone. The library of over 3,000 games included Gran Turismo (the best-seller with 10.85 million units), Final Fantasy VII (which moved the series from Nintendo to Sony), and Crash Bandicoot. The DualShock controller (1997), with two analog sticks and vibration motors, became the industry standard. Square and Enix, attracted by CD-ROM capabilities, migrated from Nintendo to Sony — a shift that redefined the RPG landscape.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePlayStation redefined the industry through CD-ROM as a standard medium, through branding the video game platform as an adult product, and through the controller model that persists to this day. Production continued for 12 years (1994–2006), and PSone (2000) brought a redesigned, more compact version. In 2006, NASA chose the PS1 processor for the New Horizons probe due to its demonstrated reliability.",
