        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 1990s, Sega dominated the 16-bit console market with Genesis, but was under immediate pressure from the transition to a new generation. Sony was preparing PlayStation, and the arcade industry was evolving rapidly toward 3D graphics — Virtua Fighter (1993) had demonstrated that audiences demanded authentic three-dimensional experiences. Sega needed to respond with a console capable of reproducing at home the 3D arcade titles from its own lineup.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eSaturn began development in 1992 under the coordination of Hideki Sato. The project initially focused on a Hitachi SH-1 processor, but the architecture was expanded by adding a second VDP for improved 2D performance. The decision to use two Hitachi SH-2 32-bit RISC processors at 28.6 MHz added significant complexity. The final configuration included eight processors distributed across four subsystems: CPU, Video, Audio, and CD-ROM — an architecture difficult to program but powerful for 2D. The use of quadrilaterals instead of triangles as basic graphical primitives created difficulties for ports from other platforms.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eSega of America organized a surprise launch at E3 1995, announcing immediate availability at $399 — four months ahead of the planned schedule. Sony immediately countered: $299 for PlayStation. Sega\u0027s decision alienated retailers not included in the limited launch and left third-party developers without prepared games. The launch library was limited to six Sega titles.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eSaturn sold 9.26 million units globally — significantly less than PlayStation. However, the console dominated in Japan, where arcade culture was strong, and offered near-perfect arcade ports from Capcom and SNK, supported by the 4 MB RAM expansion cartridge. Titles like NiGHTS into Dreams, Panzer Dragoon, and Virtua Fighter 2 demonstrated unique capabilities. The absence of a 3D Sonic game (Sonic X-treme was cancelled in 1997) was widely considered a critical strategic error.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eSaturn maintains cult status thanks to its remarkable library of 2D and experimental games. The Model 2 controller is considered by many one of the best console controllers ever made. The introduction of the analog stick through the 3D Pad anticipated the standard that would become universal. The dedicated community continues to celebrate the platform for its originality and fighting game library.",
