        "advantages":  [
                           "First with analog stick as standard feature.",
                           "Four player ports integrated natively.",
                           "Nearly nonexistent loading times thanks to cartridges.",
                           "Trilinear filtering for smooth textures and anti-aliasing.",
                           "Library with high-quality games and major innovations."
                       ],
        "disadvantages":  [
                              "Limited capacity: 64 MB cartridge vs 650 MB CD.",
                              "High production costs raised game prices.",
                              "Alienation of third-party developers (Square, Enix migrate to Sony).",
                              "Small library: only 388 official titles.",
                              "Aggressive compression for textures and sound, no FMV."
                          ],
        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the mid-1990s, PlayStation had radically changed the market through CD-ROM adoption and attracting major studios. Nintendo, with SNES approaching retirement, needed to respond with a 64-bit console that would demonstrate 3D graphical superiority. Silicon Graphics (SGI), a leader in high-performance computing, offered the right technology to bring supercomputer graphics to consumer products.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo 64 began development in 1993 under the codename \u0027Project Reality\u0027, through a partnership with SGI. The final architecture included the NEC VR4300 processor based on MIPS R4300i at 93.75 MHz and the Reality Coprocessor at 62.5 MHz — a single chip that handled both graphics and audio. The system included 4 MB RDRAM expandable to 8 MB through the Expansion Pak, with unified memory architecture. N64 was the first console with native trilinear filtering and anti-aliasing. The controversial decision to remain with ROM cartridges (versus CD-ROM) offered nearly nonexistent loading times and anti-piracy protection, but alienated major studios due to high production costs.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eN64 debuted in Japan on June 23, 1996, in North America on September 29, 1996, and in Europe on March 1, 1997. Super Mario 64 as the pack-in title pioneered 3D camera control and navigation in three-dimensional spaces — a conceptual leap from previous 2D games. The controller with standard analog stick and expansion port for Rumble Pak and Controller Pak redefined interaction with 3D games.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eN64 sold 32.93 million units globally, dominating the North American market but losing to PlayStation globally. The library of 388 titles was relatively small but exceptionally high-quality: Super Mario 64 (11.62 million), The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time, GoldenEye 007 (which revolutionized console shooters), Mario Kart 64, and Perfect Dark. Square and Enix migrated to Sony due to cartridge limitations — a significant loss for the catalog.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eN64 pioneered camera control in 3D games, the analog stick as a standard, four-player local multiplayer, and 16-bit audio at 44.1 kHz with Dolby Pro Logic. Ocarina of Time remains one of the highest-rated games in industry history. Its legacy continues through modern emulation and FPGA hardware like Analogue 3D.",
