        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the late 1990s, Game Boy dominated the portable market with an overwhelming share, but competition from Game Gear and Lynx had shown that audiences wanted color. The success of Pokémon Red and Blue (1996) had created a massive Game Boy user base demanding more. Nintendo needed to respond with color without abandoning the existing user base — a delicate compatibility challenge.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eGame Boy Color was designed as a careful evolution of Game Boy, not a new platform. The Sharp LR35902 processor was doubled to 8.39 MHz from the original\u0027s 4.19 MHz. RAM increased to 32 KB, and a palette of 56 colors (32 simultaneous) replaced the green-gray tones. Screen size remained identical at 160×144 pixels. The crucial decision was complete backward compatibility: all original Game Boy games ran on GBC, many receiving automatic or specific color enhancements.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eGame Boy Color was launched in October 1998 in Japan and November 1998 in North America at $79.99. Pokémon Gold and Silver were released simultaneously as GBC exclusives — a strategic move that ensured immediate adoption. The infrared port allowed data exchange between consoles without a cable.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eGame Boy Color sold over 32 million units of its own, bringing the total Game Boy line to over 118 million combined. Pokémon Gold/Silver sold over 23 million copies. Backward compatibility allowed Game Boy users to adopt GBC without abandoning their existing games — a rare industry decision that rewarded user base loyalty.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eGame Boy Color demonstrated that a gradual evolution with backward compatibility is more commercially effective than a radical break. The lesson would be applied again with Game Boy Advance (2001), which maintained compatibility with GBC and Game Boy. Pokémon Gold/Silver as an exclusive killer app showed that a single major title can validate a new platform almost instantly.",
