        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the mid-1990s, Sega was in crisis. Saturn had sold only 9.26 million units compared to PlayStation\u0027s over 100 million. Sega\u0027s reputation had been damaged by the failures of Sega CD and 32X, and retailers and consumers had become cautious toward the company\u0027s products. PlayStation 2 was announced for 2000 and promised superior graphical power with integrated DVD. Sega needed to launch a new console as quickly as possible — ideal against anticipated competition or even ahead of it.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eDreamcast was developed in parallel by two rival teams — the US team proposed Hitachi SH-4 with 3dfx Voodoo 2, while the Japanese team supported the same CPU with NEC PowerVR2. Sega chose the Japanese design, generating a dispute with 3dfx resolved later. The design philosophy changed fundamentally from Saturn: standard components close to PC architecture, not special-purpose components. The Hitachi SH-4 processor at 200 MHz, coupled with NEC PowerVR2 at 100 MHz capable of 3–7 million polygons per second, offered solid performance. The integrated 33.6k or 56k modem, VMU (Visual Memory Unit) with its own LCD screen, and Windows CE/DirectX support completed an innovative package.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eDreamcast was launched in Japan in November 1998, with PowerVR chipset manufacturing problems and limited stock. The legendary North American launch on September 9, 1999 — the date that became iconic \u00279/9/99\u0027 — at $199 generated 225,000 units sold and $98.4 million in the first 24 hours. Europe received the console on October 14, 1999. Sonic Adventure, Soul Blade, and NFL 2K were the launch titles.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eDreamcast reached 1 million units sold in North America by October 2000. Sonic Adventure sold 2.5 million copies. Soulcalibur, Shenmue (with a record budget of $50–70 million), Phantasy Star Online (the first online RPG on console), and Jet Set Radio (innovative cel-shaded graphics) defined the library. However, the PlayStation 2 announcement with integrated DVD, the absence of Electronic Arts support, and piracy facilitated by the MIL-CD exploit rapidly eroded prospects. On January 31, 2001, Sega announced the discontinuation of Dreamcast production.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eDreamcast pioneered online gaming on console, concepts that would become Xbox Live and PlayStation Network. VMU remains one of the most creative peripheral innovations in industry history. Isao Okawa, Sega\u0027s chairman, forgave the company\u0027s debts to him personally and returned his shares worth $695 million before his death in 2001, helping the company survive the transition to third-party developer. Even in 2026, enthusiasts maintain private servers for Phantasy Star Online.",
