        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe monochrome Neo Geo Pocket (1998) had proven that SNK could create competitive portable hardware, but the monochrome screen had placed the console at an immediate disadvantage against Game Boy Color. The color portable market was growing rapidly, and SNK needed to respond with a color version to remain relevant. Sega Dreamcast had just been launched and offered a cross-platform connectivity opportunity.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo Pocket Color retained the base hardware of the monochrome model — the Toshiba TLCS-900H processor at 6.144 MHz, 12 KB RAM — adding a 160×152 pixel color LCD screen with 146 colors from a 4,096-color palette. The microswitch joystick with distinct tactile feel was retained, maintaining the control advantage over the standard D-pad. Compatibility with monochrome Neo Geo Pocket cartridges was complete. The Dreamcast connectivity feature allowed data transfer and exclusive content between consoles.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo Pocket Color was launched in March 1999 in Japan and in August 1999 in North America at $69.99. Exclusive titles like SNK vs. Capcom: Match of the Millennium, The King of Fighters R-2, and Sonic the Hedgehog Pocket Adventure were strong selling points.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo Pocket Color sold approximately 1.5 million units — more than the monochrome model, but incomparable to Game Boy Color\u0027s 32+ million. Dreamcast connectivity created a unique but niche synergy. SNK went bankrupt in 2001, ending platform support.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNeo Geo Pocket Color remains appreciated by collectors for build quality, the microswitch joystick, and the fighting game library. SNK vs. Capcom: Match of the Millennium is considered one of the best fighting games for a portable console. The platform demonstrates that a technically and qualitatively excellent product can fail commercially if it appears too late in an already consolidated market.",
