        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the late 1990s, the Japanese portable market was dominated by Game Boy Color. Bandai, a major toy manufacturer in Japan, wanted to enter this market with a differentiated product. Gunpei Yokoi — the creator of Game Boy, who had left Nintendo — designed for Bandai a radical handheld with dual orientation, ultra-thin form factor, and minimal power consumption.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eWonderSwan was designed by Gunpei Yokoi with the philosophy \u0027less is more\u0027: the NEC V30MZ processor at 3.072 MHz, 16 KB RAM, and a custom GPU produced 224×144 monochrome pixels. The ultra-slim dimensions of 10.3 cm width, rotatable orientation design (portrait and landscape), and operation on a single AA battery for over 30 hours of battery life defined the value proposition. WonderSwan could be held vertically or horizontally, with buttons on both sides — an ergonomic innovation.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eWonderSwan was launched in Japan in March 1999 at 4,800 yen — cheaper than Game Boy Color. Exclusive Bandai titles (including Final Fantasy I and II ports, Gundam) were the main attraction in the Japanese market. The console was never officially launched outside Japan.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eWonderSwan sold over 3 million units in Japan — a notable performance for a monochrome console in 1999. It gained a significant market share in the Japanese market against Game Boy Color, impressing with its affordable price and design. The lack of international localization and Nintendo\u0027s global dominance limited expansion beyond Japan\u0027s borders.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eWonderSwan remains an example of innovative design limited by restrictive commercial strategy. The dual orientation — vertical and horizontal — anticipated concepts that would be adopted by Nintendo DS and portrait versions of modern mobile devices. WonderSwan Color (2000) and SwanCrystal (2002) followed, but could not fundamentally alter the global portable market balance.",
