        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 2000s, Game Boy Advance dominated the portable market but was technically limited. Sony was preparing PSP — a portable console with hardware close to PlayStation 2 — directly threatening Nintendo\u0027s dominance in the portable segment. Nintendo, under Satoru Iwata\u0027s leadership, chose not to compete on specifications but to innovate the concept of game interaction, addressing new audiences who had never played before.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo DS was designed as a radical reinvention of the portable console concept: dual screen with a resistive touchscreen on the bottom, a digitizer that allowed stylus input. The configuration included two processors — ARM9 at 67 MHz and ARM7 at 33 MHz — 4 MB RAM, integrated Wi-Fi, and a microphone. The upper screen (256×192) and the lower touchscreen (256×192) allowed simultaneous display of different content. Compatibility with GBA cartridges was complete through a dedicated slot.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo DS was launched in North America in November 2004 and in Japan in December 2004 at $149.99. Nintendo intentionally positioned DS as a \u0027third pillar\u0027 alongside Game Boy Advance and GameCube — not as a direct successor. Brain Age (Dr. Kawashima\u0027s Brain Training) transformed DS into a cultural phenomenon, selling over 35 million copies and attracting an adult audience that had never played video games.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo DS sold 154.88 million cumulative units (DS, DS Lite, DSi, DSi XL) — the second best-selling Nintendo console after Wii. DS Lite (2006) with improved brightness and DSi (2008) with integrated digital cameras kept the platform relevant. Pokémon Diamond/Pearl, Mario Kart DS, and New Super Mario Bros. were titles with sales exceeding 10 million each. Wi-Fi Connection introduced accessible online multiplayer on a portable console.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo DS demonstrated that interaction innovation can surpass competitors\u0027 technical advantages. PSP, with superior hardware, failed to match DS in the global portable market. The dual screen concept and touchscreen directly influenced Nintendo 3DS design and anticipated the touch interfaces that would become standard on smartphones and tablets.",
