        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 2000s, Nintendo dominated the portable market through Game Boy Advance, without a serious competitor for many years. Sony, after the massive success of PlayStation 2, wanted to extend the PlayStation brand to the portable segment. The objective was not just a game console, but a complete multimedia device — a \u0027Walkman of the 21st century\u0027 — combining games, music, photos, and internet in a single elegant device.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePSP was designed as a performance device: MIPS R4000 processor at 333 MHz, 32 MB RAM (64 MB from PSP-2000 version) and a Graphics Core GPU at 166 MHz with graphical capabilities close to PlayStation 2. The 4.3-inch widescreen TFT LCD at 480×272 pixels in 16:9 format was remarkable for a portable device in 2004. Sony introduced Universal Media Disc (UMD) — a proprietary 1.8 GB optical format — as the primary storage medium. Wi-Fi, NetFront web browser, and Remote Play function completed the multimedia package.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePSP was launched in Japan in December 2004 and in North America in March 2005 at $249.99. Launch titles included Ridge Racer, Spider-Man 2, and Metal Gear Acid. The platform evolved through five models: PSP-1000 (original), PSP-2000 Slim (19% thinner, 33% lighter), PSP-3000 (improved screen), PSP Go (no UMD, 16 GB internal storage), and PSP-E1000 Street (budget, no Wi-Fi).\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePSP sold over 80 million units — a considerable commercial success, but less than Nintendo DS\u0027s 154+ million. The library included AAA titles: God of War: Chains of Olympus, Grand Theft Auto: Liberty City Stories, Metal Gear Solid: Peace Walker, and Gran Turismo. Piracy through Custom Firmware drastically affected developer revenues. The iPhone\u0027s appearance (2007) fundamentally changed public perception of portable gaming, eliminating the need for a dedicated device for many consumers.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePSP demonstrated that the home console experience was possible on a portable device. It introduced multimedia convergence on gaming-dedicated hardware and pioneered Remote Play — streaming games from PS3 to PSP. PSP\u0027s lessons directly influenced PlayStation Vita (2011), which attempted to correct its predecessor\u0027s strategic mistakes.",
