        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the mid-2000s, PlayStation 2 had dominated an entire generation with 160+ million units. Sony entered the new generation with extraordinary technological ambition: a revolutionary processor, integrated Blu-ray format, and a platform that would define digital entertainment for a decade. Microsoft had launched Xbox 360 a year ahead, creating pressure for a rapid PS3 launch. The HD format war — Blu-ray vs. HD DVD — offered Sony an opportunity to win through widespread PS3 adoption.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePlayStation 3 was built around the Cell Broadband Engine processor — a $400 million investment, developed in partnership with IBM and Toshiba. Cell included a Power Processing Element (PPE) core at 3.2 GHz and seven active Synergistic Processing Elements (SPE), reaching 204.8 theoretical GFLOPS. The NVIDIA RSX \u0027Reality Synthesizer\u0027 GPU, 256 MB GDDR3 VRAM, and 256 MB Rambus XDR DRAM completed the configuration. Blu-ray (50 GB per disc versus 8.5 GB DVD) offered enormous capacity and positioned PS3 as the cheapest Blu-ray player on the market at launch. The Cell architecture proved extremely difficult to program, leading to initial versions of third-party games inferior to those on Xbox 360.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePS3 was launched in Japan and North America in November 2006 at prices of $499 (20 GB) and $599 (60 GB) — the highest in Sony console history. Sony sold at a loss due to Cell and Blu-ray costs. Early versions included PS2 emulation hardware for complete compatibility, later removed for cost reasons. Uncharted: Drake\u0027s Fortune (2007) defined the platform\u0027s identity for cinematic games.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePS3 sold 87.4 million units globally. Blu-ray won the HD format war against HD DVD, partly thanks to PS3 adoption. PlayStation Network offered free online multiplayer compared to Xbox Live Gold. The April 2011 security breach compromised 77 million accounts and shut down PSN for several weeks. The Last of Us (2013) pushed the narrative boundary of video games.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003ePS3 won the HD format war through Blu-ray and pioneered the PlayStation Plus subscription model with monthly free games — a concept later adopted by the entire industry. The Cell processor, though initially difficult to program, enabled remarkable graphical experiences once developers learned the architecture. The exclusive library — Uncharted, God of War III, The Last of Us — defines the standards for cinematic games to this day.",
