        "history":  "\u003cstrong\u003eHistorical Context\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eIn the early 2010s, Nintendo DS had dominated the portable market with 154+ million units. Sony was preparing PlayStation Vita with PS3-level hardware in portable format. Nintendo couldn\u0027t win a specifications race against Vita and didn\u0027t want to — instead, it sought a perceptual innovation that would differentiate 3DS from any competitor: a glasses-free 3D screen.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsole Development\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo 3DS used ARM11 MPCore processors at 268 MHz and a PICA200 GPU for 3D rendering. The autostereoscopic 3D display technology — a parallax barrier that directs light rays separately to each eye — allowed three-dimensional effects without special glasses. 3D depth was adjustable or fully disableable to eliminate eye discomfort. Camera, gyroscope, accelerometer, and StreetPass — passive data exchange between nearby consoles — completed an innovative ecosystem. Compatibility with DS games was complete.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo 3DS was launched in Japan in February 2011 and in North America in March 2011 at $249.99. The price was dramatically reduced to $169.99 in August 2011 — after six months — due to initial sales below expectations. Super Mario 3D Land and Mario Kart 7 reinvigorated the platform at the end of 2011. New 3DS (2014) brought a faster processor and automatic 3D stabilizer.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMarket Impact\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo 3DS sold 75.94 million cumulative units, finishing the generation as the third best-selling portable console in Nintendo history. The library included Animal Crossing: New Leaf (12+ million), Pokémon X/Y, Monster Hunter (a phenomenon in Japan), Fire Emblem: Awakening, and The Legend of Zelda: A Link Between Worlds. StreetPass created a unique social dimension — passive data exchange between players on public transport or at events.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLegacy\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eNintendo 3DS demonstrated that perceptual innovation can differentiate a product even if specifications are inferior to the competition. The platform extended the portable segment in the post-smartphone era through experiences that couldn\u0027t be replicated on phones. The transition to Nintendo Switch (2017) marked the end of the dedicated handheld era for Nintendo, but 3DS remains appreciated for its title diversity and StreetPass social dimension.",
